.TH RASPIBACKUPCONFIG 1 "@DATE@" "raspiBackup @VERSION@" "raspiBackup Manual"
.SH NAME
raspiBackupConfig \- configure, install and uninstall raspiBackup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B raspiBackupConfig
.br
.B raspiBackupConfig
.BR \-i " | " \-m " | " \-u " | " \-U
.RB [ \-t
.BR crond | systemd ]
.br
.B raspiBackupConfig
.BR \-h | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B raspiBackupConfig
is a menu driven tool to configure the most important options of
.BR raspiBackup (1)
and to set up regular backups.
Usually
.B raspiBackup
is installed with
.BR raspiBackupInstall.sh ,
which starts
.B raspiBackupConfig
at the end.
The menu doesn't offer an installation.
.PP
For users not familiar with Linux the options
.BR \-i ,
.B \-U
and
.B \-u
install, update and uninstall
.B raspiBackup
without the need to know the required
.BR apt-get (8)
commands.
.PP
.B raspiBackupConfig
writes the configuration file
.I /usr/local/etc/raspiBackup.conf
(see
.BR raspiBackup.conf (5)).
.PP
Called without options it starts an interactive
.BR whiptail (1)
user interface with the following main menu entries:
.TP
.B M1 About and useful links
Version information and links to the documentation.
.TP
.B M2 Configure major options
Configure the language, backup path, backup type, backup mode, number of backups to keep
or smart recycle, services to stop and start, partitions to back up,
compression, message detail, eMail notification, clone device and the
regular backup (enable or disable it, weekday and time).
.TP
.B M3 Update components
Update
.B raspiBackup
or
.BR raspiBackupConfig .
.TP
.B M4 Uninstall raspiBackup
Uninstall
.B raspiBackup
with
.BR apt-get (8)
and remove the apt repository.
.PP
Regular backups are run either by a
.BR systemd (1)
timer or by
.BR cron (8).
systemd is used if it is available, unless a cron configuration already
exists or
.B \-t
is given.
.PP
.B raspiBackupConfig
has to be run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Display a short help text and exit.
.TP
.B \-i
Unattended install of
.BR raspiBackup :
add the signed
.B raspiBackup
apt repository, install the Debian package
.B raspibackup
with
.BR apt-get (8)
and create a default configuration.
.TP
.B \-m
Add new options of the configuration template installed by the Debian package
.B raspibackup
to
.I /usr/local/etc/raspiBackup.conf
and comment out options which no longer exist, as described for
.BR \-U .
Nothing is done if the configuration is up to date or
.B raspiBackup
isn't installed as Debian package.
The package calls this option when it's updated.
.TP
.BR \-t " crond" | systemd
Use cron or systemd for regular backups.
.TP
.B \-u
Unattended uninstall of
.B raspiBackup
with
.BR apt-get (8)
and removal of the apt repository.
.TP
.B \-U
Unattended update of
.B raspiBackup
with
.BR apt-get (8)
to the latest version.
A version kept by reverting to the previous version in the update menu
isn't updated.
Afterwards new options of the configuration template are added to
.I /usr/local/etc/raspiBackup.conf
and options which no longer exist are commented out.
Existing values are kept.
The previous configuration is saved as
.IR raspiBackup.conf.v<version> .
If
.B raspiBackup
isn't installed as Debian package, only
.B raspiBackupConfig
is updated.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the version and exit.
.SH NOTES
If
.B raspiBackup
is installed as Debian package
.BR raspibackup ,
the option
.B \-i
is not available.
The package is updated with
.B raspiBackupConfig \-U
or with
.BR apt (8):
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade
.fi
.RE
.PP
In both cases the package updates the configuration with
.BR "raspiBackupConfig \-m" .
If the configuration is still older than the installed configuration template,
.B raspiBackupConfig
offers to update it when it's started.
.PP
When the package is removed, the systemd units and the cron file created
by
.B raspiBackupConfig
are removed as well.
.SH APT COMMANDS
All actions of
.BR raspiBackupInstall.sh ,
.BR "raspiBackupConfig \-i" ,
.BR \-u " and " \-U
and of the update menu can be done manually with
.BR apt (8)
as well.
.SS Install the package
Download the signing key and check that its fingerprint is
.BR 23A2E977F377FC31CE44DC9C4B9E02DBACA4DD24 .
Don't continue if it differs.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B curl \-fsSL \-o /tmp/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg \e
.B "    https://framps.github.io/raspiBackup/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg"
.B gpg \-\-show\-keys /tmp/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install the key, add the repository and install the package:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo install \-m 644 \-D /tmp/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg \e
.B "    /usr/share/keyrings/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg"
.B sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/raspibackup.sources <<EOF
.B Types: deb
.B URIs: https://framps.github.io/raspiBackup
.B Suites: stable beta
.B Components: main
.B Signed\-By: /usr/share/keyrings/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg
.B EOF
.B sudo apt update
.B sudo apt install raspibackup
.fi
.RE
.SS Upgrade the package
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt update
.B sudo apt upgrade
.fi
.RE
.PP
To upgrade only
.BR raspiBackup :
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt update
.B sudo apt install \-\-only\-upgrade raspibackup
.fi
.RE
.SS Install a beta
Betas are installed only on request. Once a beta is installed, upgrades
install newer betas and finally the next release.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt update
.B sudo apt install \-t beta raspibackup
.fi
.RE
.SS Uninstall a beta
Revert to the current release:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt update
.B sudo apt install raspibackup/stable
.fi
.RE
.SS Revert to a previous release
If an upgraded release doesn't work, list the available versions,
install the previous one and hold it.
Without the hold the next upgrade installs the broken release again.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt update
.B apt list \-a raspibackup
.B sudo apt install \-\-allow\-downgrades raspibackup=\fIversion\fP
.B sudo apt\-mark hold raspibackup
.fi
.RE
.PP
When a fixed release is available release the hold and upgrade:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt\-mark unhold raspibackup
.B sudo apt update
.B sudo apt upgrade
.fi
.RE
.SS Delete the package
Remove the package and its configuration, then the repository and its key:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B sudo apt purge raspibackup
.B sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/raspibackup.sources \e
.B "    /usr/share/keyrings/raspibackup\-archive\-keyring.gpg"
.B sudo apt update
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/raspiBackup.conf
Configuration of
.BR raspiBackup .
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/raspibackup/raspiBackup_{en,de}.conf
Configuration templates.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/raspiBackup.{service,timer}
systemd units for regular backups.
For other intervals than daily or weekly edit the timer manually.
.TP
.I /etc/cron.d/raspiBackup
cron file for regular backups.
.TP
.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d/raspibackup.sources
.br
.I /usr/share/keyrings/raspibackup-archive-keyring.gpg
apt repository of
.B raspiBackup
and its signing key.
.TP
.I ./raspiBackupConfig.log
Log file, written into the current directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR raspiBackup (1),
.BR raspiBackup.conf (5),
.BR systemd.timer (5),
.BR crontab (5)
.PP
Full documentation:
.UR https://raspibackup.linux-tips-and-tricks.de/en/home/
.UE
